            <ArticleTitle>Hypothalamic astrocytes in obesity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>57-66</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ecl.2012.11.003</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0889-8529(12)00127-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Obesity is characterized by a chronic and low-grade inflammation in tissues including the hypothalamus. Hypothalamic inflammation is considered an early and determining factor for the onset of obesity, a factor that occurs even before body weight gain. Within the hypothalamus, microglia and astrocytes produce cytokines that drive inflammatory responses. Astrocytes are directly affected by nutrient excess and might play a unique role in promoting hypothalamic inflammatory responses in obesity. This article reviews evidence supporting the role of hypothalamic astrocytes in obesity, and suggests a new approach for neuroendocrine research designed to reveal pathogenesis and develop novel treatment strategies against obesity.</AbstractText>
